          "course": "Anglais",
          "passage": "Both security and insecurity reside in national, international and local contexts which determine the conditions of living. They contribute either to the welfare of communities and individuals or to the rivalities between them. In Africa national institutions have remainde fixated on territorial and regime security. The majority of the citizens have been, forr th\" most part, left to fend themselves in clan and ethnic enclaves which are frequentely not secure. Recent cases of insecurity are clear. In the late 2002 the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC) eastern borders have been fragile. Ethnic confrontation between Hema and Lendu tribes lde thrm to insecurity. The repeated incursions of LRA rebals from Uganda greatly destabilizednthe northeastern border of the DRC. In 2009 an agreement was signed between the DRC and the Republic of Rwanda putting an end to the border conflict.\n\nThe dominant picture of marginality in Africa is represebted by the notion of borderlands. These are vast uncaptured spaces where people live insecure between the existing states. Borderlands unite thr region in a system of localized insecurities. They are seen from the Ogaden of the Ethiopia - Somalia border, northeastern Kenya on the Ethiopia and Somalia borders, and northwestern Kenya, bordering Ethiopia, Sudan and Uganda. Borderlands are also marginal in ecological and economic terms. In the way that they are home to the fragile pastoralist economies which are alwals subject to drougth, desertification, floods and food insecurity. Over the years borderlands have been home to some of the largest refugee camps in Central Africa -Impfondo, Betou in the Republic of Congo, Zemio in the Central African Republic; in eastern Africa - Daadab and Kakuma in northern Kenya.\n\nThroughout the decade 1980 and 1990, the droughts and famine that existed in the region brought resource conflits that contributed to the vulnerability of communities. Over time, conflicts opposing pastorals and faming communities have reached the neighbouring areas. For instance environmental conflicts in northern Kenya that had involed the Somali community began to affect the Orma pastoralists and Pokomo farming communities in the Tana River district.\n\nIn the center of the Rift Valley, land and resource disputes among the Kalenjin, Kikuyu, Kisii ans Masai have become more common. These conflicts are frequenty increased by politicians during times of elections which reflect real resource contestations that are not going to disappeae any time soon. Competition over scare resources remained after the post - electoral violence that affected this region in late 2007 and 2008. It continues to retard the return of internally displaced persons to their homes.\n\nThe United Nations Children's Fund (UNICEF) estimated that well over 100, 000 children are in need of urgent therapeutic care for severe malnutrition in the borderlands. In the meanwile 6 millio children are in danger of malnutrition which exposes them to famine and illness in Ethiopia. The agency also estimated that 3.4 million Ethiopians will need food aid over the next three months.\n\nIn the refugee camps high population growth faces a fragile ecosystem. It engenders more enviroemental degradation. According to conservation international eastern Africa is one of the most degraded ecosystems in the world. There remains only 5 percent of its original habita. Wiyth more political instablility, this environment invariably produces endemic food insecurity. A multilateral forum was held in Nairobi in June 2007 in order to launch a campaign to combat food shortages. The participants to the forum recognized that least 20 millionpeople need emergency aid.\n\nQUESTIONS ON THE TEXT",
